WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.040
Another legacy Marvel character may be about
to come bubbling to the surface of the MCU.

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.700
A scene early in Avengers: Endgame seems like
it could be a hidden tease for the impending

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.980
arrival of Namor the Sub-Mariner, one of Marvel's
oldest characters.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.640
An anti-hero with an 80-year publication history,
Namor has served as both a nemesis and an

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.720
ally to a laundry list of iconic Marvel heroes.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.000
Spoilers for Avengers: Endgame to follow,
obviously.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.800
Fans have long wondered whether Namor the
Sub-Mariner would ever make an appearance

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.180
in the Marvel Cinematic Universe, and Endgame
may have actually given us our answer… kind

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:34.680
of.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.840
During the scene in question, Scarlett Johansson's
Natasha Romanoff is convening a holographic

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.000
council of heroes, who are scattered after
the five-year time jump.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.200
One of the participants is Okoye, who appears
to be holding Wakanda together in the absence

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.740
of the dusted King T'Challa.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:55.000
She reports seismic activity on the floor
of the Atlantic Ocean, under the African plate,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.440
which apparently falls under Wakanda's jurisdiction.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:01.980
When asked by Romanoff how she intends to
handle it, Okoye says, "It's an earthquake

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:02.900
under the ocean.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.780
We handle it by not handling it."

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.040
At first it seems like a throwaway line, but
if we know Marvel, it was included for a purpose

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.000
— and we have a couple key pieces of evidence
to back up our assertion.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.150
The fact that the disturbance was reported
by Okoye is telling, as Wakanda and Atlantis

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.600
have a bit of a history in the comics — that
is to say, they don't get along, and have

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.180
actually gone to war on more than one occasion.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:30.840
Not only that, in the second issue of the
original Infinity Gauntlet miniseries, an

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.220
undersea earthquake occurs in the wake of
Thanos's snap — and none other than Namor,

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.440
the Sub-Mariner, is on hand to investigate.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.320
More importantly than all that, though, is
the fact that Marvel Studios is well-known

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.450
for "planting seeds" which suggest the possibility
of characters that might up come into play

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.840
down the line, and this particular seed was
not the first one that's been planted in the

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.380
MCU concerning Namor.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:56.720
Way back in 2010's Iron Man 2, there was a
scene in which Nick Fury and Tony Stark discussed

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:00.380
tentative plans to scout for the team that
would soon become the Avengers.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.860
During their conversation, Fury was referring
to a series of holographic screens, many of

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.399
which depicted events from the past and the
future in the MCU.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:13.100
But one screen was simply a world map which
showed various "hot spots" around the globe

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.900
which had garnered the interest of S.H.I.E.L.D.,
and one of these spots happened to be located…

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:20.060
smack in the middle of the Atlantic Ocean.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.780
Of course, it's almost certain that at the
time that particular Easter egg was dropped,

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.620
nobody had any idea whether Namor would ever
come into play in the MCU.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:34.060
But the inclusion of Okoye's report in Avengers:
Endgame suggests that Marvel Studios might've

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:39.800
kept the character in mind this entire time...and
that they may now be prepping us for his formal

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:40.420
introduction.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.020
Speaking of introductions...

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:47.580
Namor first appeared in Motion Picture Funnies
Weekly #1, a promotional publication handed

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.980
out to movie theaters all the way back in
April 1939.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.540
This book was, in fact, the very first to
ever be published by the company that would

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:01.360
later become Marvel Comics, making Namor not
just a legacy character, but the legacy character.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.371
Namor's origin story and characteristics are
quite similar to that of DC's Aquaman, who

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.260
wouldn't appear until November of 1941.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:14.450
The half-human son of an Atlantean princess,
he's a prince and later king of that underwater

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:17.240
realm whose mistrust of the surface world
runs deep.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.640
He's super-strong, super-tough, and — yes
— he can also communicate with marine life.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.240
So yeah...in all ways that matter, he's basically
the original Aquaman.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.280
But this guy can fly.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.100
"That was the worst pep talk.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:31.940
Ever."

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:36.400
Given the huge success Warner Bros. saw with
Aquaman, it wouldn't be a huge stretch for

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.950
Marvel Studios to try and show their superheroic
rivals their own take on an undersea adventure.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.950
Meanwhile, with the death of Iron Man, the
retirement of Captain America, and the departure

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:50.250
of Thor for space with the Guardians of the
Galaxy, Marvel still needs some action to

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.160
go down on earth.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:56.300
What better character to bring onto the scene
than their original superguy, Namor?

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:01.120
Not only that, but Marvel finally has access
to the Fantastic Four, now that Disney has

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.860
acquired 20th Century Fox.

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:07.720
Considering Namor's deep ties to the FF in
the comics, the time might finally be right

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.400
for Marvel's undersea king to show the surface
world who's boss.

